**Test filters.** For this step these gates run only the tests their filter selects (plus any inherited expected-red tests); put the tests this step adds or changes where a filter value finds them:
- `constructor-unit-tests`: 

**Declared actions.** This step MUST do exactly these, and the worker base prompt says what each verb obliges:
- `ADD_TEST OpticalConstructor.Tests.ExperimentCopyTests`

**How to implement.** Copying experiments from another kit with the data files they reference and the snapshots they need (001 §H 'Between kits'; .spec-md §3.15: S6 completes the copy with items and explicit mapping before its closing composition; the S6 brief: each experiment is copied with its setup files, the data files it references (into data/, under S4.2's collision rule, hashes re-recorded) and the library snapshots it needs; clashing names get S4.1's suffix rule). An S4.3 experiment carries its setup inside its own file (the `setup` array, step 146) and references no `.setup-json`, so copying the experiment file copies its setup. In `OpticalConstructor.Storage/KitContentFiles.fs` (steps 146-149), because the copy reads and writes experiment files through its typed members, each doc one to three lines citing spec 0050 S6: (1) `ExperimentCopyReport = { experiments : Experiment list; dataFiles : DataAttachment list; snapshots : LibraryVersionRef list }`: each copied experiment as written, each data file copied in and each snapshot added. (2) `type KitContext with member this.copyExperimentsFrom (source : KitContext) (ids : ExperimentId list) : Result<ExperimentCopyReport, KitLibraryError>` reads everything it needs before it writes anything, in this order. (a) Under `ReadOnlyKitAccess` it answers `KitLibraryKitErr (KitReadOnlyErr "copyExperimentsFrom")` and reads nothing. (b) It reads `source.proxy.readExperiments ()` (step 148) and keeps, in the source's order, the experiments whose id `ids` names; an id the source lacks is `KitLibraryKitErr (MissingKitFileErr <its ExperimentKitFile file name>)`. (c) It lists `source.proxy.listSnapshots ()` (step 138) and takes `referenceClosure` (step 203) of the kept experiments' `libraryReferences` (step 137), in order, resolving an `EntryVersionReference` to the source's snapshot of that version and an `EntryLatestReference` to the source's highest-version snapshot of that entry; a reference the source holds no snapshot of is `UnheldEntryErr` naming its `describe`. Of that closure it keeps the versions `this.proxy.listSnapshots ()` holds no snapshot of. (d) It reads, through `source.proxy.readLabFile` (step 204), each distinct `dataFileOpt` path of the kept experiments, in order of first appearance. (e) It renumbers the kept experiments with `Experiment.renumberedFor` (step 203) over the ids of `this.proxy.readExperiments ()`. (f) It writes, in this order: each kept snapshot through `this.proxy.writeSnapshot` (step 138), byte for byte the source's entry file; each data file through `this.proxy.copyInFile <folder> source.file.leafName source.content` (step 133), where `<folder>` is `KitDataFolder.tryCreate` (step 132) of the path's `parentOpt` (step 196) when that answers `Ok`, and `KitDataFolder.root` otherwise, so a file keeps its data folder, takes the next free suffix on a clash (step 132's rule), and its new attachment (`KitDataFile.attachment`, step 132) records the hash of the bytes the kit now holds; and each renumbered experiment, its `dataFileOpt` replaced by the new attachment of its source path, through `this.proxy.writeExperiment` (step 148). The snapshots and data come first, so a written experiment never references a file the kit lacks. A `KitError` is `KitLibraryKitErr`; the first failure stops the copy, and what it wrote stays. (g) It answers the report. It pins nothing in the library: a version arrives as the source's snapshot, as a received kit's does, and the kit's import dialog resolves it the next time the kit is opened (step 141); a version the kit already holds keeps the kit's snapshot (step 139's rule). The source is only read. This first copy operation is extended with item transfer and mapping by steps 227-229 within S6, before the section's closing composition. No merge, deduplication of equal files across folders, or cache is added. Tests: new `OpticalConstructor.Tests/ExperimentCopyTests.fs`, `module ExperimentCopyTests`, appended at the end of the compile list with no comment. Its kits are `KitProxy.createInMemory` over fresh `InMemoryKitFolder`s (step 125), initialised with test-local kit files and opened through `KitContext.openKit` with a stepping clock, the source read-only and the target read-write unless a fact says otherwise. The source is filled before its read-only open: its experiments recorded through `{ kit = <it>; imports = <an import context over BuiltinCatalogue.freshStores ()> }.recordExperiment` (step 149), so its snapshots are the library's, and its data files placed through `placeLabFile` and attached through `attachLabFile` (step 134). E1 binds `SampleIds.multilayerQwId` at version 1 and `PresetIds.source600` by its entry id and attaches `data/raw/run 1.csv`; E2 binds `SampleIds.glassPlate1mmId` at version 1 and attaches nothing; E3 binds nothing and attaches `data/b.csv`. `a copy brings exactly the chosen experiments, the data files they reference and the snapshots they need`: copying [E1] into an empty target answers a report of E1, its one data file and E1's closure, and leaves the target's `paths` exactly `.kit-json`, `data`, `data/raw`, `data/raw/run 1.csv`, `library` and the `library/<name>` of each version of E1's closure; `readExperiments` answers E1 with an attachment at `data/raw/run 1.csv` whose hash is that of the source's bytes; the source's `paths` and texts are unchanged. `a copied experiment whose id the kit holds takes the next free id`: over a target holding E1 and E2 (written through `writeExperiment`), copying [E2; E3] leaves `readExperiments` answering the target's E1 and E2 unchanged, the source's E3 as E3, and the source's E2 as E4. `a copied data file whose name the kit holds takes the next free suffix, and its hash is re-recorded` : over a target holding `data/raw/run 1.csv` with other text, copying [E1] attaches `data/raw/run 1 (2).csv` with the hash of the source's bytes, and the target's first file keeps its text; with the source's file rewritten by an outside writer after its attach, the copied attachment records the hash of the rewritten bytes. `a data file two copied experiments share is copied once`: a source E5 that attaches `data/raw/run 1.csv` as E1 does, copied with E1, writes one data file, and both copied experiments reference it. `a snapshot the kit already holds is kept and not written again`: over a target already holding one version of E1's closure (written through `writeSnapshot`), copying [E1] reports every other version of the closure and not that one, and the target lists it once. `a copy whose data file the source lacks writes nothing` : a source experiment whose attached file an outside writer removed (`removeLabFile`, step 133) makes the copy answer `KitLibraryKitErr (MissingKitFileErr _)`, and the target's `paths` are unchanged. `a kit opened read-only copies nothing` : over a target opened read-only, the copy answers `KitLibraryKitErr (KitReadOnlyErr _)` and its `paths` are unchanged. `an experiment the source does not hold is refused and nothing is written`: copying [E9] answers `KitLibraryKitErr (MissingKitFileErr _)` naming `E9.experiment-json`, and the target's `paths` are unchanged.

**Acceptance.** After the copy lands, `KitContext.copyExperimentsFrom` MUST write exactly the chosen experiments, renumbered on a clash, the data files they reference with re-recorded hashes, and the source snapshots of their closure the kit lacks, reading everything before writing and the source only read; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `a copy brings exactly the chosen experiments, the data files they reference and the snapshots they need`, `a copied experiment whose id the kit holds takes the next free id`, `a copied data file whose name the kit holds takes the next free suffix, and its hash is re-recorded`, `a data file two copied experiments share is copied once`, `a snapshot the kit already holds is kept and not written again`, `a copy whose data file the source lacks writes nothing`, `a kit opened read-only copies nothing` and `an experiment the source does not hold is refused and nothing is written`, the count rising by 8; and build, elevation and the fourteen s22 invariants MUST pass.
